  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA quasi-stationary front is currently located across the\nregion. There is a warm and very humid airmass also across the \nregion. Widely scattered showers and storms are redeveloping \nthis afternoon with some help from a weak lake breeze and old \noutflow boundaries too. A broad upper level trough has setup \nover the Great Lakes region. Around the base of this upper level\ntrough will bring weak disturbances to aid in the development \nof scattered showers and storms. A round of scattered convection\nis expected to develop this afternoon for areas of NEOH and \nNWPA. This round will move eastward through the evening. Another\nround of scattered convection will develop over NWOH later this\nevening and track eastward across northern Ohio into NWPA \novernight. A couple strong to severe storms may be possible with\ndamaging wind gusts as the main threat. There is a marginal \nrisk (level 1 of 5) highlighted from SPC for the rest of today. \n\nWe will start off the day Saturday fairly quiet. But the quasi-\nstationary front will push through as as weak cold front during\nthe afternoon with additional scattered showers and\nthunderstorms possible. Weak high pressure will build into the\narea Saturday night and Sunday with a drier forecast. High\ntemperatures will be in the 80s and overnight lows in the 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe weather pattern becomes more unsettled for the early part of\nnext week. A broad upper level trough will continue to be over\nthe Great Lakes region for early next week. Another cold front\nwill drop down through the Great Lakes on Monday and slow down\nas it moves into our area. Multiple rounds of convection will be\npossible along this slow moving or quasi-stationary boundary\nMonday through Wednesday. SPC has highlighted the area in a Day\n4 severe weather outlook on Monday. The main severe weather \nthreat will be damaging wind gusts and large hail. Heavy \nrainfall will also be a concern for Monday into Tuesday. WPC has\nthe area highlighted in a slight risk for excessive rainfall \nboth Monday and Tuesday. Timing of these multiple rounds of \nconvection is a little uncertain at this time. Widespread \nseveral inches of rainfall is very possible Monday through \nWednesday. The quasi-stationary front may finally push south of\nthe area by Thursday into Friday with lower rain chances and\nslightly cool, less humid air.",
